Chapter: HdaAudio
  cites Foreword chapter Board
  grounds Device.Mmio

 The HDA controller sits at MMIO 0xFE000000 (PCI slot 2). Output is
 stream descriptor 0: a Buffer Descriptor List (BDL) names PCM buffers,
 and setting the stream's RUN bit hands them to the codec's DAC. codex-vm
 drains the BDL to the host's waveOut when RUN is written, so a program
 that fills a buffer with samples and runs the stream is heard on the
 speakers or headphones.

 This is output only. codex-vm models one output stream and no capture
 stream -- there is no ADC path that delivers host microphone samples --
 so Microphone.listen has no seam on this machine and is not implemented
 here. The format is 48 kHz 16-bit stereo, little-endian signed PCM,
 which is what the host waveOut is fixed at.

 We say:

Section: Registers

  hda-bar : Integer = #FE000000
  hda-reg-gctl : Integer = 8
  hda-reg-sd0ctl : Integer = 128
  hda-reg-sd0cbl : Integer = 136
  hda-reg-sd0lvi : Integer = 140
  hda-reg-sd0fmt : Integer = 146
  hda-reg-sd0bdpl : Integer = 152
  hda-reg-sd0bdpu : Integer = 156

  hda-sample-rate : Integer = 48000
  hda-fmt-48k-16-stereo : Integer = 17
  hda-run-bit : Integer = 2
  hda-amplitude : Integer = 8000

Section: Tone Buffer

  hda-square-tone : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hda-square-tone (freq) (nsamples) = hda-square-tone-at freq nsamples hda-amplitude

  hda-square-tone-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hda-square-tone-at (freq) (nsamples) (amp) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes (nsamples * 4)
    in let w = hda-tone-into buf freq nsamples amp
    in buf

  hda-tone-into : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hda-tone-into (buf) (freq) (nsamples) (amp) =
    let period = if freq <= 0 then 109 else hda-sample-rate / freq
    in hda-tone-fill buf period nsamples amp 0

 The negative half is the two's complement of the positive one in 16 bits,
 which is what the codec reads: an amplitude of zero has to stay zero on
 both halves rather than wrapping to 65536.

  hda-tone-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hda-tone-fill (buf) (period) (n) (amp) (i) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let phase = int-mod i period
    in let neg = if amp <= 0 then 0 else 65536 - amp
    in let s = if phase * 2 < period then amp else neg
    in let lo = bit-and s 255
    in let hi = bit-and (bit-shru s 8) 255
    in let w0 = poke-byte buf (i * 4) lo
    in let w1 = poke-byte buf (i * 4 + 1) hi
    in let w2 = poke-byte buf (i * 4 + 2) lo
    in let w3 = poke-byte buf (i * 4 + 3) hi
    in hda-tone-fill buf period n amp (i + 1)

Section: Play

 The controller reads the descriptor list and the samples AFTER the call
 that starts the stream has returned, so both must outlive the PLAYBACK and
 not merely the call. A caller whose heap is rewound on a frame boundary has
 to own them; `hda-play-pcm-at` is for that caller, and it is the same reason
 the network send path must not be bracketed by a heap restore.

 **A frame-scoped BDL is a DMA read of freed memory**, and this is a lifetime
 obligation that is invisible at the call site, which is why it is written
 here rather than left to be noticed. It does not fault where the mistake is:
 the call returns cleanly, the allocator hands the same bytes to something
 else, and the controller streams whatever landed there. Pass a descriptor
 list and a buffer that live at least as long as the sound.

  hda-play-pcm-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  hda-play-pcm-at (bdl) (buf) (len) =
    let w0 = poke-32 bdl 0 buf
    in let w1 = poke-32 bdl 4 0
    in let w2 = poke-32 bdl 8 len
    in let w3 = poke-32 bdl 12 1
    in act
      a <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-gctl) 1
      b <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd0bdpl) bdl
      c <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd0bdpu) 0
      d <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd0lvi) 0
      e <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd0cbl) len
      f <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd0fmt) hda-fmt-48k-16-stereo
      g <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd0ctl) hda-run-bit
      g
    end

  hda-play-pcm : Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  hda-play-pcm (buf) (len) = hda-play-pcm-at (alloc-bytes 16) buf len

  hda-play-tone : Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  hda-play-tone (freq) (ms) =
    let nsamples = hda-sample-rate * ms / 1000
    in hda-play-pcm (hda-square-tone freq nsamples) (nsamples * 4)

  hda-stop : [Audio] Integer
  hda-stop = mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd0ctl) 0

 The controller counts every PCM buffer it drains to the DAC and exposes
 the running total at BAR offset #180. Nothing inside a running guest can
 hear its own audio, so a streaming test plays a known number of buffers
 and reads this counter to prove each one reached the output -- the same
 way the SMP tests read a cell only a second core writes. Read-only.

  hda-reg-drain-count : Integer = #180

  hda-drained-count : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  hda-drained-count = mmio-read-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-drain-count)

Section: Capture (input stream 1, the microphone)

 Input is the mirror of output: a BDL names a buffer, and setting input
 stream 1's RUN bit hands the buffer to codex-vm, which copies the most
 recent microphone samples into it (from its waveIn ring) and clears RUN.
 The guest polls RUN back to zero and reads the captured PCM. The first
 capture opens the mic and returns near-silence -- the ring has not filled
 yet -- so a real reading takes a warm-up capture first.

  hda-reg-sd1ctl : Integer = 160
  hda-reg-sd1cbl : Integer = 168
  hda-reg-sd1lvi : Integer = 172
  hda-reg-sd1fmt : Integer = 178
  hda-reg-sd1bdpl : Integer = 184
  hda-reg-sd1bdpu : Integer = 188

  hda-capture : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  hda-capture (len) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes len
    in let bdl = alloc-bytes 16
    in let w0 = poke-32 bdl 0 buf
    in let w1 = poke-32 bdl 4 0
    in let w2 = poke-32 bdl 8 len
    in let w3 = poke-32 bdl 12 1
    in act
      a <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-gctl) 1
      b <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd1bdpl) bdl
      c <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd1bdpu) 0
      d <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd1lvi) 0
      e <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd1cbl) len
      f <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd1fmt) hda-fmt-48k-16-stereo
      g <- mmio-write-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd1ctl) hda-run-bit
      done <- hda-wait-capture 200000
      buf
    end

  hda-wait-capture : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  hda-wait-capture (fuel) = act
    c <- mmio-read-32 (hda-bar + hda-reg-sd1ctl)
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else if bit-and c 2 == 0 then 0
    else hda-wait-capture (fuel - 1)
  end

  hda-peak : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hda-peak (buf) (nsamples) = hda-peak-loop buf nsamples 0 0

  hda-peak-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hda-peak-loop (buf) (n) (i) (mx) =
    if i >= n then mx
    else let lo = peek-byte buf (i * 4)
    in let hi = peek-byte buf (i * 4 + 1)
    in let raw = lo + hi * 256
    in let val = if raw >= 32768 then raw - 65536 else raw
    in let a = if val < 0 then 0 - val else val
    in hda-peak-loop buf n (i + 1) (if a > mx then a else mx)
